  [38;5;153msetUser[39m, [38;5;153mgetUserId[39m, [38;5;153misLoggedIn[39m, [38;5;153mclear[39m. It owns the storage key, the [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m read/write, and the single
  definition of "nobody is logged in" ([38;5;153mnull[39m). [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns the userId; the submit handler hands it to [38;5;153mAppSession[39m.
  Future forms only ever call [38;5;153mAppSession.getUserId()[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m one place to change storage, one place for the not-logged-in answer, and consumers that never touch
  [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m directly. [3mCosts:[0m one new file and a global. [3mComplexity:[0m trivial-to-moderate.

  [1mB. Storage-key convention, no module.[0m Agree the key is [38;5;153mapp.userId[39m; each consumer calls
  [38;5;153msessionStorage.getItem("app.userId")[39m itself.
  [3mBuys:[0m zero new files, nothing to learn. [3mCosts:[0m the key is duplicated at every call site, so a typo fails silently as
  "logged out"; each consumer invents its own missing-value handling; and switching storage later means editing every
  consumer. [3mComplexity:[0m trivial.

  [1mC. Session module with subscriptions.[0m A, plus [38;5;153mAppSession.subscribe(fn)[39m so already-rendered forms react to login and
  logout.
  [3mBuys:[0m correctness for UI that's on screen before login happens. [3mCosts:[0m a pub/sub and teardown story for a need that
  doesn't exist yet — there is no second form and no logout. [3mComplexity:[0m moderate.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m B's cost lands exactly where you said you're heading — "other forms will need it later" is the case
  where a duplicated string key and per-consumer null handling start biting. C is real engineering for a subscriber
  that doesn't exist; if a form ever needs to react live, adding [38;5;153msubscribe[39m to A is additive and breaks no caller. A is
  the smallest thing that makes the [3mnext[0m form easy.

  Three things I'll flag now because they shape the design, not the choice:

  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub[0m that returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m and never calls the API. A real userId comes from the
    server, so the stub has to synthesize one. I'll derive it from the username with an explicit stub marker, so
    swapping in the real API changes one function body and nothing else.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[39m can throw[0m (disabled storage, some private-browsing modes). Unguarded, that turns a storage problem
    into a broken login button. The module will catch and fall back to in-memory.
  - [1mFailure path.[0m The session must only be set when [38;5;153msuccess[39m is true. The stub always succeeds today, so this is
    currently unreachable code — worth writing correctly anyway rather than retrofitting.

  Does A look right, and are those three flags the behavior you want?

